        ex:node_4_f97800eb_b8fe_4903_9ebf_91dbcf4cafd2_18,
        ex:node_4_f97800eb_b8fe_4903_9ebf_91dbcf4cafd2_19,
        ex:node_4_f97800eb_b8fe_4903_9ebf_91dbcf4cafd2_20,
        ex:node_4_f97800eb_b8fe_4903_9ebf_91dbcf4cafd2_9 ;
    neo:id "dbf2271b472de2f5170babde36f5e59f"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_name "250_BCE_-_Dhammacakkappavattana_Sutta_Four_Noble_Truths_Wikipedia_translation.md"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_slug "four-noble-truths"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:paragraph_number 4 ;
    neo:sentence_number 1 ;
    neo:text "Nirodha (cessation, ending, confinement): the attachment to this transient world and its pain can be severed or contained by the confinement or letting go of this craving;"^^xsd:string .

ex:node_4_f97800eb_b8fe_4903_9ebf_91dbcf4cafd2_2 a neo:Document ;
    neo:MENTIONS ex:node_4_f97800eb_b8fe_4903_9ebf_91dbcf4cafd2_3,
        ex:node_4_f97800eb_b8fe_4903_9ebf_91dbcf4cafd2_4,
        ex:node_4_f97800eb_b8fe_4903_9ebf_91dbcf4cafd2_5,
        ex:node_4_f97800eb_b8fe_4903_9ebf_91dbcf4cafd2_6 ;
    neo:id "3b810954c4a5bf545c822fd21729a30d"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_name "250_BCE_-_Dhammacakkappavattana_Sutta_Four_Noble_Truths_Wikipedia_translation.md"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_slug "four-noble-truths"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:paragraph_number 2 ;
    neo:sentence_number 1 ;
    neo:text "Dukkha (not being at ease, 'suffering') is an innate characteristic of transient existence; nothing is forever, this is painful;"^^xsd:string .

ex:node_4_f97800eb_b8fe_4903_9ebf_91dbcf4cafd2_21 a neo:Document ;
    neo:MENTIONS ex:node_4_f97800eb_b8fe_4903_9ebf_91dbcf4cafd2_12,
        ex:node_4_f97800eb_b8fe_4903_9ebf_91dbcf4cafd2_22,
        ex:node_4_f97800eb_b8fe_4903_9ebf_91dbcf4cafd2_23,
        ex:node_4_f97800eb_b8fe_4903_9ebf_91dbcf4cafd2_24,
        ex:node_4_f97800eb_b8fe_4903_9ebf_91dbcf4cafd2_3 ;
    neo:id "81d56105ed01e3e1f81e0c6bcd4bfaf1"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_name "250_BCE_-_Dhammacakkappavattana_Sutta_Four_Noble_Truths_Wikipedia_translation.md"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_slug "four-noble-truths"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:paragraph_number 5 ;
    neo:sentence_number 1 ;
    neo:text "Marga (road, path, way): the Noble Eightfold Path is the path leading to the confinement of this desire and attachment, and the release from dukkha."^^xsd:string .

ex:node_4_f97800eb_b8fe_4903_9ebf_91dbcf4cafd2_7 a neo:Document ;
    neo:MENTIONS ex:node_4_f97800eb_b8fe_4903_9ebf_91dbcf4cafd2_10,
        ex:node_4_f97800eb_b8fe_4903_9ebf_91dbcf4cafd2_11,
        ex:node_4_f97800eb_b8fe_4903_9ebf_91dbcf4cafd2_12,
        ex:node_4_f97800eb_b8fe_4903_9ebf_91dbcf4cafd2_13,
        ex:node_4_f97800eb_b8fe_4903_9ebf_91dbcf4cafd2_8,
        ex:node_4_f97800eb_b8fe_4903_9ebf_91dbcf4cafd2_9 ;
    neo:id "92dae8a38ee0f2d0b695d114ce098fe1"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_name "250_BCE_-_Dhammacakkappavattana_Sutta_Four_Noble_Truths_Wikipedia_translation.md"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_slug "four-noble-truths"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:paragraph_number 3 ;
    neo:sentence_number 1 ;
    neo:text "Samudaya (origin, cause): together with this transient world and its pain, there is also thirst (desire, longing, craving) for and attachment to this transient, unsatisfactory existence;"^^xsd:string .

